00:12:47,660 --> 00:12:51,260
Behold, the humble corkscrew.

198
00:12:52,980 --> 00:12:57,840
Count this simple mechanical device among the iconic gadgets that have withstood the test of time.

199
00:12:58,600 --> 00:13:00,860
Corkscrews have a fascinating history.

200
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:03,660
They actually were inspired by guns.

201
00:13:03,661 --> 00:13:13,280
The guns in the Middle Ages came with a worm attached for extracting a bullet from the muzzle,

202
00:13:13,281 --> 00:13:17,780
and that was the inspiration behind the original corkscrews.

203
00:13:17,781 --> 00:13:22,320
Beer and cider containers like these were the first to have their corks pulled

204
00:13:22,321 --> 00:13:24,320
with a screw in the 17th century.

205
00:13:24,321 --> 00:13:28,320
Today, a pneumatic corkscrew can also do the job.

206
00:13:28,321 --> 00:13:34,320
The bottling of wine didn't happen until the late 1700s, early 1800s.

207
00:13:34,321 --> 00:13:38,320
Once wine was bottled, corks had to be flush-driven.

208
00:13:38,321 --> 00:13:41,320
They had to be inserted all the way into the neck.

209
00:13:41,321 --> 00:13:47,120
So once you had bottled wine, you de facto had to have a corkscrew.

210
00:13:47,121 --> 00:13:50,440
But not all corkscrews are created equal.

211
00:13:50,441 --> 00:13:56,140
There are two types, the auger and helix.

212
00:13:56,141 --> 00:14:02,460
The helix consists of a metal spiral, usually comprised of at least five rings.

213
00:14:02,461 --> 00:14:06,920
When turned, the point of the helix warms its way into the cork, displacing as little

214
00:14:06,921 --> 00:14:10,420
of the cork material as possible.

215
00:14:10,421 --> 00:14:16,180
The nauger is a screw with a solid shaft that, when turned, drills into the cork.

216
00:14:16,181 --> 00:14:20,420
This motion can tear at the cork tissue and possibly release debris into the wine.

217
00:14:20,421 --> 00:14:26,660
A good corkscrew, simple as it is, needs to be a well-designed gadget, or frankly,

218
00:14:26,661 --> 00:14:30,540
you just shred the cork to smithereens.

219
00:14:30,541 --> 00:14:32,921
The Culinary Institute of America in St.

220
00:14:32,922 --> 00:14:35,660
Helena, California, is home to one of the most

221
00:14:35,661 --> 00:14:38,460
of the most famous corkscrew collections in the world,

222
00:14:38,461 --> 00:14:42,300
with over 1,800 devices.

223
00:14:42,301 --> 00:14:45,000
The structure was once the Christian Brothers Winery,

224
00:14:45,001 --> 00:14:48,540
where beginning in 1949, its legendary cellar master,

225
00:14:48,541 --> 00:14:51,500
