228
00:14:57,141 --> 00:14:58,180
of this gadget.

229
00:14:58,181 --> 00:14:59,820
He wanted to know the most efficient way

230
00:14:59,821 --> 00:15:02,280
of opening a bottle of wine.

231
00:15:02,281 --> 00:15:05,180
A lot of these corkscrews come in a whole variety of shapes

232
00:15:05,181 --> 00:15:10,100
sizes and many of these are novelty corkscrews. As you can see we have a

233
00:15:10,101 --> 00:15:14,900
monk looking very pious and sanctimonious here but you remove his head and he's got

234
00:15:14,901 --> 00:15:19,520
a corkscrew under there. These are examples of pocket corkscrews. What we

235
00:15:19,521 --> 00:15:25,820
have here are folding bows. They are single pull corkscrews as well as double

236
00:15:25,821 --> 00:15:30,720
finger pulling corkscrews. We also have a little left-handed corkscrew that the

237
00:15:30,721 --> 00:15:33,720
goes in the opposite direction that it would for a right-handed

238
00:15:33,721 --> 00:15:36,720
person. By the late 19th century,

239
00:15:36,721 --> 00:15:40,720
the corkscrew became part of almost any multipurpose tool,

240
00:15:40,721 --> 00:15:43,720
from folding pocket gadgets to this precursor of the Swiss

241
00:15:43,721 --> 00:15:47,720
Army knife. I mean, look at these.

242
00:15:47,721 --> 00:15:51,720
These have not only a corkscrew, a hammer.

243
00:15:51,721 --> 00:15:55,720
It is a ruler with actual inch increments here.

244
00:15:55,721 --> 00:15:58,720
I mean, if you're not drinking wine, you're building a house.

245
00:15:58,721 --> 00:16:03,720
In addition to wine, corkscrews have opened everything from medicine to cosmetic bottles.

246
00:16:03,721 --> 00:16:08,720
You'd hardly imagine that there would be a bottle of wine for this corkscrew,

247
00:16:08,721 --> 00:16:11,720
but this is actually used to remove corks from perfume bottles.

248
00:16:11,721 --> 00:16:16,720
The waiter's friend is perhaps the most popular of all corkscrews,

249
00:16:16,721 --> 00:16:21,720
and one that's used in the wine service classes taught at the Culinary Institute.

250
00:16:21,721 --> 00:16:24,720
There is a correct way to use a corkscrew.

251
00:16:24,721 --> 00:16:29,760
screw. First of all, you want to take out your knife, make sure it's nice and sharp.

252
00:16:29,761 --> 00:16:31,876
Cut below the second lip of the bottle.

253
00:16:31,877 --> 00:16:34,480
This way when you do get that foil off, you make

254
00:16:34,481 --> 00:16:39,960
sure that there's not going to be any foil in your wine. Next, you take the worm and

255
00:16:39,961 --> 00:16:44,266
go directly down into the center of the bottle.

256
00:16:44,267 --> 00:16:48,480
The first step up is a nice light pull. Second

257
00:16:48,481 --> 00:16:50,820
The first step is the same.

258
00:16:50,821 --> 00:16:53,620
Now ease that cork out with your own hand,

259
00:16:53,621 --> 00:16:56,620
nice and slowly, very gently.

260
00:16:56,621 --> 00:16:59,020
And now you're ready to enjoy your wine.

261
00:16:59,021 --> 00:17:02,620
But the trustworthy corkscrews days may be numbered,

262
00:17:02,621 --> 00:17:06,940
thanks to new bottle openers like the air-pressured cork lifter.

263
00:17:06,941 --> 00:17:13,180
You want to be very careful here because there's a lot of air pressure going in with each pump.

264
00:17:13,181 --> 00:17:16,780
Cork screws have a precarious future.

265
00:17:16,781 --> 00:17:21,687
more wines are bottled under screw cap that don't require a corkscrew.

266
00:17:21,688 --> 00:17:23,580
Here's a gadget that may in

267
00:17:23,581 --> 00:17:28,700
a hundred years from now disappear and that would be sad indeed.

268
00:17:28,701 --> 00:17:31,260
While the corkscrew's future may

269
00:17:31,261 --> 00:17:36,700
look cloudy, the umbrella is one tried-and-true gadget that's weathered the tides of time.

270
00:17:38,620 --> 00:17:43,980
The umbrella was introduced 4,000 years ago in China when its primary use was to provide

271
00:17:43,981 --> 00:17:48,640
shade from the sun. The Chinese were also the first to waterproof their umbrellas by

272
00:17:48,641 --> 00:17:50,674
using lacquer and wax coatings.

273
00:17:50,675 --> 00:17:54,480
In the 16th century, the umbrella was introduced in Europe

274
00:17:54,481 --> 00:17:58,280
to shade women with fair complexions and strong arms.

275
00:17:58,281 --> 00:18:02,620
Umbrellas weighed 10 pounds or more a piece because they had this heavy cloth and the

276
00:18:02,621 --> 00:18:07,485
heavy stays that were either whalebone or baleen.

277
00:18:07,486 --> 00:18:11,060
And then in 1852, the U-shaped steel

278
00:18:11,061 --> 00:18:15,980
stay was invented which is the the thing that holds the cloth out the weight

279
00:18:15,981 --> 00:18:19,580
drops so that its popularity increased tremendously.

280
00:18:19,581 --> 00:18:26,480
Gary Durrell of Okemos Michigan is an expert on this gadget. He owns one of the

281
00:18:26,481 --> 00:18:31,640
world's largest private collections of antique umbrellas. I have hundreds I

282
00:18:31,641 --> 00:18:36,660
think that they're a lost collectible and that there are very few people who

283
00:18:36,661 --> 00:18:42,600
collect them. Some of Gary's umbrellas date from the 18th century and many offer

284
00:18:42,601 --> 00:18:47,420
more than just protection from inclement weather like this German pipe umbrella.

285
00:18:47,421 --> 00:18:52,680
They're all gadgets in terms of the mechanism to make the umbrella but what

286
00:18:52,681 --> 00:18:57,460
I like best are the gadget umbrellas which means they do something. This is my

287
00:18:57,461 --> 00:19:04,740
favorite gadget umbrella. It has a compass in the ivory handle and you take

288
00:19:04,741 --> 00:19:14,040
Take the handle off and it has a telescope the entire length of the umbrella and with

289
00:19:14,041 --> 00:19:19,980
very powerful optics and dates 1780 to 1800.

290
00:19:19,981 --> 00:19:25,480
This is the French gun umbrella from the 1880s and this is how it works.

291
00:19:25,481 --> 00:19:29,300
By taking the handle off you can put a shell in there and this would probably be a large

292
00:19:29,301 --> 00:19:35,700
like a shotgun, and then you take the ferrule out in order to cock and shoot the gun.

293
00:19:35,701 --> 00:19:40,000
And so then to shoot it, all you need to do is push up on the firing pin.

294
00:19:40,001 --> 00:19:43,000
This is another one of my favorite gadget umbrellas.

295
00:19:43,001 --> 00:19:48,800
All you need to do is pull out its three-foot sword blade if you need to be protected.

296
00:19:48,801 --> 00:19:58,000
Umbrella handles of concealed drinking cups, engraved snuff boxes, and even ladies' compacts.

297
00:19:58,001 --> 00:20:01,020
This is my rarest umbrella.

298
00:20:01,680 --> 00:20:03,080
This is called a servant umbrella.

299
00:20:03,720 --> 00:20:07,760
And then the servant would stand in the back in the rain or the sun.

300
00:20:08,160 --> 00:20:13,320
And then the person of wealth would stand underneath it as the servant would carry it like this.

301
00:20:15,060 --> 00:20:21,340
Over the years, umbrellas have gotten smaller, more waterproof, and easier to open.

302
00:20:22,280 --> 00:20:25,260
And those in the future may be able to tell you when they're needed.

303
00:20:25,261 --> 00:20:33,940
The ambient forecasting umbrella has a built-in wireless receiver that gets weather updates and flashes when rain is expected.

304
00:20:36,280 --> 00:20:40,980
Perhaps the only gadget that's seen more ups and downs than the umbrella is the zipper.

305
00:20:45,240 --> 00:20:49,760
Prior to the zipper, fabric closure devices ranged from the button to lacing.

306
00:20:49,761 --> 00:20:58,760
Then in 1851, Elias Howe, inventor of the sewing machine, created an ingenious alternative.

307
00:20:58,761 --> 00:21:02,760
He called it the Automatic Continuous Clothing Closure.

308
00:21:02,761 --> 00:21:07,760
It closed fabric with a series of small clasps that joined when a string was pulled.

309
00:21:07,761 --> 00:21:10,760
But design flaws derailed its popularity.

310
00:21:10,761 --> 00:21:18,760
In 1914, Swedish engineer Gideon Sundback patented a more functional version by more than doubling the number of teeth per inch.

311
00:21:18,761 --> 00:21:24,300
teeth per inch, creating what he called the separable fastener.

312
00:21:24,301 --> 00:21:29,360
The zipper as we know it had finally arrived.

313
00:21:29,361 --> 00:21:32,360
A zipper is composed of two opposing rows of teeth,

314
00:21:32,361 --> 00:21:35,360
each with a hook and an indentation.

315
00:21:35,361 --> 00:21:38,000
These tracks mesh when the latching mechanism known

316
00:21:38,001 --> 00:21:42,560
as the slide pulls the two strips of teeth together.

317
00:21:42,561 --> 00:21:45,060
This forces each hook into the offset indentation

318
00:21:45,061 --> 00:21:48,300
on the opposite track, securely interlocking the teeth.

319
00:21:50,560 --> 00:21:53,280
Zippers found their first use closing and opening items

320
00:21:53,281 --> 00:21:56,000
like tobacco pouches and overshoes.

321
00:21:56,001 --> 00:21:58,780
The iconic gadget finally got its familiar name

322
00:21:58,781 --> 00:22:01,700
in the 1920s when the B.F. Goodrich Company

323
00:22:01,701 --> 00:22:05,180
created the term zipper to advertise

324
00:22:05,181 --> 00:22:06,660
a new brand of galoshes.

325
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:09,300
Those zippered boots also gave us

326
00:22:09,301 --> 00:22:11,680
another popular modern term.

327
00:22:11,681 --> 00:22:14,480
The flapper has become a term associated

328
00:22:14,481 --> 00:22:18,140
with young girls in the 1920s.

329
00:22:18,141 --> 00:22:20,240
And these were modern girls.

330
00:22:20,241 --> 00:22:22,280
They were starting to really enter the workplace

331
00:22:22,281 --> 00:22:24,120
and take public transportations.

332
00:22:24,121 --> 00:22:26,640
And they were wearing these overshoes.

333
00:22:26,641 --> 00:22:30,720
And they did not always zip up their galoshes completely.

334
00:22:30,721 --> 00:22:32,760
And therefore, they would flap around.

335
00:22:32,761 --> 00:22:36,680
And they became known as flappers.

336
00:22:36,681 --> 00:22:39,280
In the 1930s, the original patent for the zipper

337
00:22:39,281 --> 00:22:43,680
expired, unzipping the floodgates for the garment industry.

338
00:22:43,681 --> 00:22:47,320
Zippers evolved from simple clothing closures to decorative fashion statements

339
00:22:47,321 --> 00:22:51,100
and are now found on everything from wetsuits to handbags.

340
00:22:53,800 --> 00:22:56,360
Manufacturing a device as small and precise as a zipper

341
00:22:56,361 --> 00:22:59,300
requires machinery with little tolerance for error.

342
00:23:00,360 --> 00:23:01,780
Position is very critical.

343
00:23:02,160 --> 00:23:05,360
Some of the die sets are down to almost a hundredth of an inch.

344
00:23:05,361 --> 00:23:08,520
If we're off a little bit, it can cause a zipper to open up

345
00:23:08,521 --> 00:23:11,020
or it can cause a zipper to be too tight and not functional.

346
00:23:11,021 --> 00:23:15,020
The UCAN Zipper Factory in Los Angeles, California

347
00:23:15,021 --> 00:23:18,020
manufactures a wide variety of zippers.

348
00:23:18,021 --> 00:23:22,020
But in America, it's an industry that's losing its zip.

349
00:23:22,021 --> 00:23:24,020
We are one of the last remaining zipper companies

350
00:23:24,021 --> 00:23:25,020
in the United States.

351
00:23:25,021 --> 00:23:28,020
A lot of companies have gone offshore into China.

352
00:23:28,021 --> 00:23:31,020
The zipper on your pants probably came from a factory

353
00:23:31,021 --> 00:23:34,020
in Chow Toh, Jaijoing Province.

354
00:23:34,021 --> 00:23:38,020
The zipper capital churns out an amazing 124,000 miles

355
00:23:38,021 --> 00:23:40,020
of them each year.

356
00:23:40,021 --> 00:23:45,020
While the zipper is secure in the pantheon of time-tested gadgets,

357
00:23:45,021 --> 00:23:49,020
high-tech tinkerers like Mike and Matt at the Stupid Fun Club

358
00:23:49,021 --> 00:23:51,020
continue to create new gadgets,

359
00:23:51,021 --> 00:23:55,020
some even with artificial intelligence and attitude.

360
00:23:55,021 --> 00:23:59,020
You have left me open for too long. Close me now.

361
00:24:01,020 --> 00:24:05,020
James Smith & Sons, England's first umbrella store,

362
00:24:05,021 --> 00:24:10,580
opened in 1830 and is still owned and run as a family business.

363
00:24:11,500 --> 00:24:14,420
Ultimate Gadgets will return on Modern Marvels.

364
00:24:15,420 --> 00:24:19,160
We now return to Ultimate Gadgets on Modern Marvels.

365
00:24:20,740 --> 00:24:22,620
Where do gadgets come from?

366
00:24:23,200 --> 00:24:26,660
Some are developed at corporate research labs or high-tech think tanks.

367
00:24:27,740 --> 00:24:30,120
But many come from more humble origins.

368
00:24:30,121 --> 00:24:33,320
In a cluttered Baltimore, Maryland basement,

369
00:24:33,900 --> 00:24:37,040
Brent Farley, who calls himself the world's greatest inventor,

370
00:24:37,500 --> 00:24:39,640
dreams of coming up with the next big thing.

371
00:24:39,641 --> 00:24:44,980
Over the years, I've probably came up with about 200 gadgets that I consider to be valid.

372
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:51,480
The problem is this 57-year-old stagehand has yet to sell even one of his concepts.

373
00:24:52,680 --> 00:24:55,260
This hasn't dampened Brent's inventive urge.

374
00:24:56,420 --> 00:24:59,720
I seem not to have any choice whatsoever in what I do.

375
00:24:59,900 --> 00:25:01,740
I feel completely and totally driven.

376
00:25:01,741 --> 00:25:05,140
I'm looking for the ideas every day, anywhere I can find them.

377
00:25:05,600 --> 00:25:07,540
Somewhere on the shelf, there's room for a new gadget.

378
00:25:07,980 --> 00:25:09,520
And this is where I'm coming into the picture.

379
00:25:09,660 --> 00:25:12,400
I don't want to say I'm a cock inventor, but I reek with imagination.

380
00:25:12,640 --> 00:25:13,500
I just reek with it.

381
00:25:14,860 --> 00:25:18,460
Many of the gadgets Brent has created are tools he uses on the job.

382
00:25:18,840 --> 00:25:22,820
This here is my gadget I call the four-in-ones.

383
00:25:22,960 --> 00:25:25,140
Crescent wrench, adjustable wrench.

384
00:25:25,660 --> 00:25:28,280
On the other end of the wrench itself receives ratchets.

385
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:30,880
There's also a Phillips screwdriver.

386
00:25:31,740 --> 00:25:35,560
and a regular screwdriver, and it hooks onto your belt.

387
00:25:35,561 --> 00:25:38,580
Everything you need is the four-in-one wrench.

388
00:25:38,581 --> 00:25:40,140
This is my self-hanging hammer.

389
00:25:40,141 --> 00:25:43,440
It's a hammer that's meant to hang up after it's used.

390
00:25:43,441 --> 00:25:47,340
It just simply goes on the user's belt and or tool bag,

391
00:25:47,341 --> 00:25:50,040
the self-hanging hammer.

392
00:25:50,041 --> 00:25:51,780
This is what I call the quick grip.

393
00:25:51,781 --> 00:25:52,860
It's magnetic.

394
00:25:52,861 --> 00:25:56,000
It's designed to be on a belt worn by the user,

395
00:25:56,001 --> 00:25:58,620
and the tape itself is recessed and cannot come off

396
00:25:58,621 --> 00:26:02,500
until it's pulled directly out of the unit.

397
00:26:02,501 --> 00:26:07,300
This is what I call the shoulder holder.

398
00:26:07,301 --> 00:26:10,380
I've used this many, many times on the job, especially going

399
00:26:10,381 --> 00:26:11,380
up and down the ladder.

400
00:26:11,381 --> 00:26:13,260
If you're on a roof, it's a great, great tool.

401
00:26:13,261 --> 00:26:14,800
Simply goes on.

402
00:26:14,801 --> 00:26:16,380
As you can see, it's hands free.

403
00:26:16,381 --> 00:26:19,560
But now when I want to use this, you have this.

404
00:26:19,561 --> 00:26:21,940
It's stretchy as long as you need it.

405
00:26:21,941 --> 00:26:22,980
And that's it.

406
00:26:22,981 --> 00:26:26,480
Now I can use it, and I just let loose.

407
00:26:26,481 --> 00:26:28,600
Wherever Brent sees a need, he creates

408
00:26:28,601 --> 00:26:29,301
gadget.

409
00:26:29,302 --> 00:26:33,800
I've had trouble with my windshield wipers through time not actually working as they should

410
00:26:33,801 --> 00:26:37,000
have. They either go this way or they flip flop and didn't clean the windshield.

411
00:26:37,001 --> 00:26:37,701
I came up with

412
00:26:37,702 --> 00:26:43,810
the zigzag windshield wiper. This is designed never to flip flop and or malfunction.

413
00:26:43,811 --> 00:26:45,000
This gadget here

414
00:26:45,001 --> 00:26:46,352
I call the wing walker.

415
00:26:46,353 --> 00:26:50,760
It's meant to give you the most radical ride you'll ever ever get riding on

416
00:26:50,761 --> 00:26:53,800
a skateboard. This here I call it the ironing net.

417
00:26:53,801 --> 00:26:56,840
Simply put your hand in, pick it up, there you go.

418
00:26:56,841 --> 00:26:57,880
You're rubbing your clothes.

419
00:26:58,480 --> 00:27:01,100
You can get around collars, get in small places.

420
00:27:01,720 --> 00:27:02,480
Call it the arning mitt.

421
00:27:02,920 --> 00:27:04,880
Here's my trash can design.

422
00:27:05,640 --> 00:27:08,380
It's meant to carry the most amount of trash

423
00:27:08,381 --> 00:27:10,500
that you could possibly carry in the most efficient way.

424
00:27:10,840 --> 00:27:13,720
This is a mini prototype, easy steering, easy mobility.

425
00:27:14,120 --> 00:27:16,060
This really utilizes your space.

426
00:27:16,061 --> 00:27:19,640
A very, very small person can carry a lot, a lot of weight

427
00:27:19,641 --> 00:27:21,260
with my trash cart.

428
00:27:22,120 --> 00:27:24,540
Some of Brent's gadgets find use in his home.

429
00:27:25,200 --> 00:27:26,480
This is my shower gadget.

430
00:27:26,840 --> 00:27:30,340
Here's the mirror. The mirror goes up and down to your desired height.

431
00:27:30,341 --> 00:27:35,340
I've got the lights on the right and left side of the face to get a really good close shave.

432
00:27:36,340 --> 00:27:39,931
I have my razor that comes in and out of my housing here that goes up and down.

433
00:27:39,932 --> 00:27:40,840
It's out of the way.

434
00:27:40,841 --> 00:27:43,840
My wash rag comes up and down and goes out of the way.

435
00:27:43,841 --> 00:27:47,340
Here's my toothpaste and here is my toothbrush.

436
00:27:47,341 --> 00:27:51,840
All within a unit and I'd hate to take a shower without it.

437
00:27:51,841 --> 00:27:59,600
When I start to get an idea, it's because I have seen something that I know I can do a little better.

438
00:28:00,820 --> 00:28:02,900
All the ideas start on paper.

439
00:28:03,160 --> 00:28:04,760
Here's a handful of drawings that I've done.

440
00:28:05,440 --> 00:28:08,540
Drawings on my self-hanging hammer, how everything starts.

441
00:28:09,060 --> 00:28:09,860
Finger hockey.

442
00:28:10,640 --> 00:28:11,620
Centrifuge bubbles.

443
00:28:12,520 --> 00:28:17,020
While Brent strives to develop one gadget that might one day make it onto store shelves,

444
00:28:17,021 --> 00:28:22,020
Across the country in a 3,000 square foot Berkeley, California

445
00:28:22,120 --> 00:28:26,660
warehouse, a pair of tinkerers dreams up gadgets that may one

446
00:28:26,760 --> 00:28:28,460
day change the way we live.

447
00:28:28,560 --> 00:28:32,060
Though they call themselves the Stupid Fun Club,

448
00:28:32,160 --> 00:28:34,880
the concepts they're exploring are far from dumb.

449
00:28:41,020 --> 00:28:45,220
The Stupid Fun Club is a think tank where we make gadgets.

450
00:28:45,221 --> 00:28:50,220
The gadgets have personalities, and our goal is to make machines very friendly to use.

451
00:28:50,221 --> 00:28:56,220
You just talk to them, they're your friends, machines talk to each other, and they'll do your bidding for you.

452
00:28:57,220 --> 00:29:04,220
Mike Winter and Matt Sabigtroth research human-machine relationships by investing gadgets with artificial intelligence.

453
00:29:06,220 --> 00:29:13,220
The key to making gadgets relate to their user is to program them with voice recognition software and language processing systems,

454
00:29:13,221 --> 00:29:16,220
enabling them to follow audio commands.

455
00:29:16,221 --> 00:29:21,220
Sir, if you'll not be needing me, I'll close down for a while.

456
00:29:21,221 --> 00:29:24,220
Very nice, Copycat. You did a great job.

457
00:29:24,221 --> 00:29:28,220
This is Mr. Cool, our refrigerator, and we just talked to it to open it.

458
00:29:28,221 --> 00:29:29,220
Cool.

459
00:29:29,221 --> 00:29:32,220
Log in by stating your first name.

460
00:29:32,221 --> 00:29:33,220
Mike.

461
00:29:33,221 --> 00:29:37,220
Hi, Mike. How are you? My wish is your command. Maybe.

462
00:29:37,221 --> 00:29:38,220
Open.

463
00:29:38,221 --> 00:29:41,220
Opening door. Mike. Recolibrating.

464
00:29:41,221 --> 00:29:44,760
Mike's intelligent appliances are also programmed with attitude.

465
00:29:45,760 --> 00:29:47,300
I'd like this nice beverage.

466
00:29:48,220 --> 00:29:49,580
What are you thinking about?

467
00:29:50,240 --> 00:29:51,320
Time is up, Mike.

468
00:29:51,640 --> 00:29:54,100
You have left me open for too long.

469
00:29:54,540 --> 00:29:55,560
Close me now.

