But if robots are the gadgets that hold the key

707
00:43:50,581 --> 00:43:51,580
to a better tomorrow,

708
00:43:51,581 --> 00:43:52,980
My name is Gats.

709
00:43:52,981 --> 00:43:55,580
they'll only serve us if we're willing to bond with them.

710
00:43:55,581 --> 00:43:57,700
Do you like talking to me?

711
00:43:57,701 --> 00:44:00,700
You're all right, I guess, for now,

712
00:44:00,701 --> 00:44:02,500
until a better one comes out.

713
00:44:02,501 --> 00:44:03,940
How old are you?

714
00:44:03,941 --> 00:44:05,700
I am two years old.

715
00:44:05,701 --> 00:44:09,140
I think speaking to this robot is much less weird.

716
00:44:09,141 --> 00:44:10,380
than typing on a keyboard.

717
00:44:10,381 --> 00:44:11,840
It's certainly more natural.

718
00:44:13,940 --> 00:44:16,780
Until that elusive better tomorrow arrives,

719
00:44:16,781 --> 00:44:19,180
our relationship with gadgets will be determined

720
00:44:19,181 --> 00:44:23,220
by their commercial success or failure.

721
00:44:23,221 --> 00:44:25,460
The marketplace ultimately decides which gadgets

722
00:44:25,461 --> 00:44:28,020
become part of our future.

723
00:44:28,021 --> 00:44:30,420
And with any luck, one of those gadgets

724
00:44:30,421 --> 00:44:34,000
may come from the basement of Brent Farley.

725
00:44:34,001 --> 00:44:36,640
I'd be very, very happy to sell one concept.

726
00:44:36,641 --> 00:44:37,360
Yes, I would.

727
00:44:37,361 --> 00:44:39,140
I'm very happy.

